# CODEOWNERS — the paths no bot may change without a human reading the diff.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (2026-09-02). Seven workflows write to this repo on a schedule —
# five open PRs against main (content-improvement, analytics-content-improvement,
# proofread, validation-fixer, votes-refresh); two push directly to branches of their own
# (keepalive to its marker file, seo-monitor to `seo-reports`) and touch nothing below.
# The paths listed here are the trust boundary of the email and auth surface: the
# Cloudflare Worker that sends as coach@bjjgraph.org, the Pages Functions that run with
# the project's environment (which today holds the Supabase service-role key — see the
# email plan's "service key in Pages" decision), the SQL that defines RLS, the workflows
# that deploy all of it and sync the secrets, and the client files that decide which
# Supabase project every visitor's browser talks to. One unreviewed line in any of them
# is a credential leak or a phishing relay, and a bot cannot tell the difference between
# a refactor and an exfiltration.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO ON ITS OWN. GitHub only enforces this file through branch
# protection: Settings > Branches > main > "Require a pull request before merging" >
# "Require review from Code Owners". Until that toggle is on, this file is documentation.
# It applies to pull requests only; a direct push to a protected branch is governed by
# the same protection rule, not by this file.
#
# Paths are matched against the repository root. Later lines take precedence, so keep
# the broad directories first and any narrower exception after them.

# The workflows, the secrets they sync, and this file itself.
/.github/                                              @diogoseca

# The digest Worker: sends mail as coach@bjjgraph.org, reads Supabase with the service key.
/workers/                                              @diogoseca

# Pages Functions: /unsubscribe (service-role writes), /l share shell, anything added later.
# Every file here runs with the Pages project's environment on every deployed branch.
/functions/                                            @diogoseca

# Schema and RLS. Applied by hand in the SQL editor, never from CI — but the repo copy is
# what the next migration is written against.
/supabase/                                             @diogoseca

# The client half of the same boundary: the auth façade (window.__bjjAuth, the OAuth
# redirect-back, pullNeural/pushNeural) and the emitter that stamps the Supabase URL and
# publishable key into every page. A change here can point the whole site at another
# project without touching a single server-side file.
/source/quartz/components/scripts/supabase.ts          @diogoseca
/source/quartz/plugins/emitters/componentResources.ts  @diogoseca
